= First-order logic — Technical =

== Signature ==
A first-order signature can contain:

* constant symbols;
* function symbols with arities;
* relation/predicate symbols with arities;
* equality.

Terms and formulas are defined recursively.

== Quantification ==
Variables range over elements of a model domain D:

<pre>
M,s ⊨ ∀x φ
</pre>

iff for every d∈D:

<pre>
M,s[x↦d] ⊨ φ
</pre>

== Completeness ==
Gödel completeness gives:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ  ⇒  Γ ⊢ φ
</pre>

for standard first-order proof systems.

Together with soundness, syntactic and semantic consequence align.

== Compactness ==
If every finite subset of Γ is satisfiable, then Γ is satisfiable.

This has powerful model-theoretic consequences.

== Löwenheim–Skolem ==
First-order theories with infinite models often have models of multiple cardinalities, limiting categorical characterization of infinite structures.

== Computability ==
First-order validity is recursively enumerable but undecidable.

This distinguishes it sharply from classical propositional logic.

